from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The first thing to do when handed a vague system design prompt is...","options":["pick a database","clarify requirements and scope","draw the final architecture","estimate server cost"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You cannot design for constraints you have not named yet - clarify functional and non-functional requirements first, see ''Approaching a design problem''."},{"prompt":"Adding more CPU and RAM to a single machine is called...","options":["horizontal scaling","vertical scaling","sharding","replication"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Scaling up one node is vertical scaling; adding more nodes is horizontal scaling."},{"prompt":"A cache mainly improves a system by...","options":["storing data permanently","reducing repeated expensive work for hot data","guaranteeing consistency","replacing the database"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A cache keeps frequently read results close and cheap to fetch, cutting load on the slower store."},{"prompt":"The CAP theorem says that during a network partition a system must give up...","options":["either consistency or availability","durability","latency","both consistency and availability"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When the network splits, you keep either strong consistency or availability, not both, see ''Consistency and CAP''."},{"prompt":"A message queue between two services mainly provides...","options":["stronger consistency","decoupling and buffering of work","faster disk reads","automatic sharding"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Queues let a producer hand off work without waiting, absorbing spikes and decoupling the two sides."},{"prompt":"Roughly, one million seconds is closest to...","options":["1 day","1.5 weeks","1 month","1 year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"1,000,000 seconds is about 11.6 days, near a week and a half - a handy anchor for back-of-envelope estimates."},{"prompt":"Storing copies of the same data on multiple database nodes is called...","options":["indexing","sharding","replication","normalization"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Replication keeps copies for read scaling and failover; sharding instead splits distinct data across nodes."},{"prompt":"Rate limiting an API protects the system by...","options":["encrypting traffic","capping how many requests a client may make in a window","compressing responses","caching results"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rate limit bounds per-client request volume so one caller cannot exhaust shared capacity."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Approaching a design problem: requirements, estimates, tradeoffs', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Turn a vague prompt into a scoped problem: list requirements, produce a back-of-envelope estimate, and name the tradeoffs you are making. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what should you do before choosing any database?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A design interview or a real project starts underspecified on purpose. Your job is to impose structure. First gather functional requirements - the concrete features the system must support, such as ''post a message'' or ''read a timeline''. Then gather non-functional requirements - targets for scale, latency, availability, and consistency. Next, estimate the load with round numbers: daily active users, requests per second, bytes stored per day. Only then sketch components, and for each choice state the tradeoff you accept. Good design is not the cleverest diagram; it is the one whose costs you can defend against the stated requirements."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Back-of-envelope for a photo service: suppose 100 million daily active users each upload 2 photos of 200 KB. That is 200 million photos per day, times 200 KB, which is 40 terabytes per day of new storage, or roughly 14 petabytes per year. Requests per second: 200 million writes spread over 86,400 seconds is about 2,300 writes per second on average, and you plan for perhaps 5x peak, near 12,000 per second. A second example, sizing reads: if reads outnumber writes 100 to 1, you are near 230,000 reads per second average - a clear signal that you must scale reads with caching and replicas, not a single database. Notice each number came from a simple product of round figures."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A chat app has 10 million daily active users each sending 40 messages per day; estimate messages per day. 2) Convert that to average messages per second (use 86,400 seconds per day). 3) If each message is about 300 bytes, estimate bytes stored per day. 4) Name one functional and one non-functional requirement for the chat app. 5) If reads are 10x writes, is caching more or less attractive? Answers: 1) 10,000,000 x 40 = 400,000,000 messages per day. 2) 400,000,000 / 86,400 is about 4,600 per second. 3) 400,000,000 x 300 bytes = 120,000,000,000 bytes, about 120 GB per day. 4) Functional: send a message to a contact; non-functional: deliver within 1 second at peak. 5) More attractive, since heavy read traffic benefits most from caching."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: jumping to a component diagram before writing down the numbers, so the design cannot be justified. Mistake: quoting a decision (''use a queue'') without stating what you trade for it (added latency and complexity). Exit: (a) Which requirement type usually drives the architecture? (b) About how many seconds are in a day, to one significant figure? Answers: non-functional requirements; roughly 90,000 seconds (86,400)."}]}'::jsonb, 'Separate functional requirements (what the system does) from non-functional requirements (how well - latency, scale, availability); the second set drives most design decisions.', 14),
  (3, 'Scaling basics: vertical vs horizontal and load balancing', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Choose between scaling up and scaling out, and explain how a load balancer distributes traffic across many servers. Warm-up: if one server maxes out at 1,000 requests per second and you need 8,000, what must you do?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Vertical scaling means giving a single machine more CPU, memory, or disk. It is simple but bounded by the largest machine you can buy and creates a single point of failure. Horizontal scaling means running many identical servers behind a load balancer, which spreads incoming requests across them using a policy such as round robin, least connections, or a hash of the client. Horizontal scaling favors stateless servers, where any request can go to any server, so shared state lives in a database or cache rather than in server memory. The load balancer also performs health checks and stops routing to unhealthy nodes."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Sizing a fleet: if each server handles 1,000 requests per second and peak demand is 12,000 per second, you need at least 12 servers, and prudent practice adds headroom for failures, so you might run 16. If one server dies, the load balancer detects the failed health check and its share, 12,000 / 16 = 750 requests per second, is spread across the survivors. Round-robin sends request 1 to server A, request 2 to server B, and so on, cycling back. A hash policy sends the same user to the same server, which helps local caching but can create hot spots if one user is very active."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Each server does 2,000 requests per second and you need 30,000; how many servers at minimum? 2) With 20 servers behind a round-robin balancer, where does the 41st request go? 3) Why do horizontally scaled web servers prefer to be stateless? 4) Name one downside of vertical scaling. 5) What does a load balancer do when a server fails a health check? Answers: 1) 30,000 / 2,000 = 15 servers minimum. 2) Back to server 1, since 41 mod 20 = 1. 3) So any request can hit any server, making it easy to add or remove nodes. 4) A single point of failure and a hard hardware ceiling. 5) It stops routing traffic to that server until it is healthy again."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: keeping session state in server memory, which breaks when the balancer sends the next request elsewhere. Mistake: forgetting failure headroom, so losing one node overloads the rest. Exit: (a) What component spreads requests across a server fleet? (b) What property lets you add servers freely? Answers: a load balancer; statelessness."}]}'::jsonb, 'Vertical scaling grows one machine and hits a hard ceiling; horizontal scaling adds machines and needs a way to spread traffic across them - that is what a load balancer does.', 13),
  (4, 'Caching strategies: where to cache and how to invalidate', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Place caches at the right layers and pick an invalidation approach that matches how fresh the data must be. Warm-up: why does caching help a read-heavy system more than a write-heavy one?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A cache stores the result of expensive work so later requests can skip it. Caches sit at many layers: in the browser, at a content delivery network near the user, in an application-level store such as an in-memory cache, and inside the database. The key metric is hit ratio, the fraction of requests served from cache. Two common patterns are cache-aside, where the application checks the cache, and on a miss reads the database and populates the cache, and write-through, where writes update the cache and database together. Invalidation keeps caches from serving stale data: set a time-to-live so entries expire, or explicitly evict a key when its underlying data changes. Naming what may go stale, and for how long, is the heart of caching."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Hit-ratio math: if a cache serves 95 percent of 100,000 reads per second, only 5,000 per second reach the database, a 20x reduction. Cache-aside flow for a user profile: request arrives, check cache for key user:42, on a miss read the row from the database, store it with a 60-second time-to-live, and return it; the next reads within 60 seconds are free. Write-through on a price update: the write sets both the database and the cache key product:7, so readers never see the old price. Estimating cache size: caching 1 million profiles at 2 KB each needs about 2 GB of memory, which fits comfortably in one cache node."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A cache with a 90 percent hit ratio faces 50,000 reads per second; how many reach the database? 2) In cache-aside, what happens on a cache miss? 3) Which pattern updates cache and database on every write? 4) You cache a stock quote that must be fresh within 5 seconds; what time-to-live fits? 5) Caching 500,000 items at 4 KB each needs about how much memory? Answers: 1) 10 percent of 50,000 = 5,000 per second. 2) Read from the database, then populate the cache before returning. 3) Write-through. 4) A time-to-live of about 5 seconds. 5) 500,000 x 4 KB = 2,000,000 KB, about 2 GB."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: caching data that must always be exact without any invalidation, so users see stale values. Mistake: a stampede, where many clients miss at once and all hit the database together; mitigate with short locks or staggered expiry. Exit: (a) What metric measures cache effectiveness? (b) Name one way to invalidate a cache entry. Answers: hit ratio; expire it with a time-to-live or explicitly evict the key on change."}]}'::jsonb, 'A cache trades freshness for speed; every caching decision is really a decision about how stale you can tolerate the data being.', 14),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The requirement type that most shapes the architecture is...","options":["functional requirements","non-functional requirements","the programming language","the team size"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Targets for scale, latency, and availability drive the structural decisions."},{"prompt":"100 million daily uploads of 200 KB each is roughly how much new storage per day?","options":["2 TB","20 TB","40 TB","400 TB"],"answer":2,"explanation":"100,000,000 x 2 photos would be 40 TB; at 1 photo each it is 20 TB - here 200,000,000 x 200 KB = 40 TB, matching the worked example."},{"prompt":"You need 12,000 requests per second and each server handles 1,000; the minimum fleet size is...","options":["6","12","120","1200"],"answer":1,"explanation":"12,000 / 1,000 = 12 servers, before adding failure headroom."},{"prompt":"Horizontally scaled web servers work best when they are...","options":["stateful","stateless","single-threaded","colocated on one machine"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Statelessness lets any request go to any server, so nodes can be added or removed freely."},{"prompt":"A cache with a 95 percent hit ratio under 100,000 reads per second sends how many reads to the database?","options":["500","5,000","50,000","95,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"5 percent of 100,000 = 5,000 reads per second reach the database."},{"prompt":"The cache-aside pattern populates the cache...","options":["on every write","on a read miss","never","only at startup"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The application reads the database on a miss, then stores the result for later reads."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Databases at scale: SQL vs NoSQL, indexing, sharding, replication', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Choose between relational and non-relational stores, speed reads with indexes, and scale a database with sharding and replication. Warm-up: if one database node cannot hold all your data, do you shard or replicate?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A relational (SQL) database enforces a fixed schema and supports joins and transactions with strong guarantees, which fits data with clear relationships. A non-relational (NoSQL) store relaxes some of this for flexible schemas and easy horizontal scaling, which fits huge volumes of simple lookups. An index is a sorted structure that turns a full table scan into a fast lookup on a chosen column, at the cost of extra space and slower writes. Sharding partitions rows across nodes by a shard key, so each node holds a slice of the data and capacity grows with nodes. Replication keeps copies of data on several nodes; a common setup routes writes to a primary and reads to replicas, improving read throughput and surviving a node loss through failover."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Indexing payoff: scanning 10 million rows to find one user is 10 million comparisons, while a balanced index finds it in about log2(10,000,000), roughly 23 comparisons - a dramatic cut. Sharding by user id: with a shard key of user_id mod 4, users land on node 0, 1, 2, or 3, so 100 million users become about 25 million per node. Read scaling with replicas: one primary plus three read replicas can serve roughly 4x the read traffic, since reads spread across four copies. Choosing a store: an inventory system with money and multi-row transactions leans SQL; a firehose of event logs keyed by id leans NoSQL."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Sharding 80 million rows evenly across 8 nodes gives about how many rows per node? 2) Roughly how many comparisons does an index need to find one row among about 1 million? 3) Which scaling technique copies the same data for read throughput and failover? 4) A workload of many-row financial transactions leans toward which store type? 5) Name one cost of adding an index. Answers: 1) 80,000,000 / 8 = 10,000,000 rows per node. 2) About log2(1,000,000), roughly 20 comparisons. 3) Replication. 4) A relational (SQL) database. 5) Extra storage and slower writes, since the index must be updated."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: choosing a shard key that concentrates traffic, creating a hot shard while others idle. Mistake: sending reads to replicas and expecting instant consistency, when replicas lag the primary. Exit: (a) What does an index trade for faster reads? (b) What splits distinct rows across nodes to grow capacity? Answers: extra space and slower writes; sharding."}]}'::jsonb, 'Sharding splits different rows across nodes to grow capacity; replication copies the same rows across nodes for reads and failover - they solve different problems and are often used together.', 15),
  (7, 'Consistency and the CAP theorem', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"State the CAP theorem plainly and decide when eventual consistency is acceptable. Warm-up: during a network partition, name the two properties you must choose between."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The CAP theorem observes that a distributed data store cannot simultaneously guarantee consistency, availability, and partition tolerance. Because real networks do partition, partition tolerance is not optional, so the practical choice during a partition is between consistency and availability. A consistency-favoring system rejects or blocks requests it cannot serve correctly, keeping data exact. An availability-favoring system answers anyway and reconciles later, accepting temporary disagreement between replicas. Strong consistency means a read always reflects the most recent write; eventual consistency means replicas may briefly differ but converge. Many systems mix models: exact for balances, eventual for a like count."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A bank transfer needs strong consistency: showing money in two places at once is unacceptable, so the system blocks during a partition rather than risk a double spend. A social feed like-count tolerates eventual consistency: seeing 1,024 versus 1,025 likes for a few seconds harms nothing, so the system stays available and converges. Estimating replica lag impact: if replicas lag up to 200 milliseconds, a read-your-own-writes feature must either read from the primary or wait, or the user may not see the comment they just posted. Choosing per feature: usernames unique means strong consistency at write; view counts mean eventual is fine."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) During a partition, which property is not negotiable in practice? 2) Which consistency model guarantees a read sees the latest write? 3) Is a trending-topics counter a good fit for eventual consistency? 4) A user posts a comment and reloads but does not see it; which consistency issue is this? 5) Name one feature that demands strong consistency. Answers: 1) Partition tolerance, since networks do fail. 2) Strong consistency. 3) Yes, brief disagreement is harmless. 4) Replica lag under eventual consistency, breaking read-your-own-writes. 5) A financial balance or a unique username check."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating CAP as a permanent one-of-three choice, when the tradeoff only bites during a partition. Mistake: demanding strong consistency everywhere, paying huge latency for data that could tolerate lag. Exit: (a) During a partition, what two properties trade off? (b) What does eventual consistency promise over time? Answers: consistency and availability; that replicas converge to the same value."}]}'::jsonb, 'Strong consistency means every read sees the latest write; eventual consistency means replicas converge after a delay - pick the weakest model your product can tolerate.', 13),
  (8, 'Message queues and asynchronous processing', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Use a message queue to decouple services, absorb spikes, and process slow work in the background. Warm-up: why hand a slow task to a queue instead of doing it inside the request?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A message queue sits between a producer that creates work and a consumer that performs it. The producer enqueues a message and returns without waiting; one or more consumers pull messages and process them at their own pace. This decouples the two sides, so a slow or failed consumer does not block the producer, and a burst of work is buffered rather than dropped. Queues enable asynchronous processing: send an email, resize an image, or update a search index after responding to the user. Delivery is usually at-least-once, so consumers must be idempotent, meaning processing the same message twice causes no harm. Workers scale horizontally by adding consumers when the backlog grows."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Absorbing a spike: a signup flow sends a welcome email that takes 500 milliseconds. Doing it inline caps throughput; enqueuing it lets the request return in a few milliseconds while workers send emails in the background. Draining a backlog: if 1,000,000 messages queue up and each consumer handles 100 per second, one consumer needs 10,000 seconds, about 2.8 hours, but 20 consumers clear it in about 500 seconds. Idempotency in action: a payment-confirmation consumer keys on the order id, so a redelivered message updates the same order once rather than charging twice. Buffering: a traffic spike to 5,000 events per second against 2,000 per second of capacity grows the queue by 3,000 per second until workers catch up."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A backlog of 600,000 messages with one consumer at 200 per second takes how long to drain? 2) How many consumers at 200 per second clear that backlog in 500 seconds? 3) Why must consumers be idempotent under at-least-once delivery? 4) Name one task well suited to a background queue. 5) What happens to a spike of work when a queue sits in front of the workers? Answers: 1) 600,000 / 200 = 3,000 seconds, about 50 minutes. 2) 600,000 / 500 = 1,200 per second needed, so 1,200 / 200 = 6 consumers. 3) Because the same message may be delivered more than once, and reprocessing must not cause harm. 4) Sending email, resizing an image, or rebuilding a search index. 5) It is buffered in the queue and drained as capacity allows, not dropped."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming exactly-once delivery and writing consumers that double-charge on redelivery. Mistake: ignoring backlog growth, so the queue balloons and latency climbs with no added consumers. Exit: (a) What property must a consumer have under at-least-once delivery? (b) What does a queue do to a burst of work? Answers: idempotency; it buffers the burst for later processing."}]}'::jsonb, 'A queue turns a synchronous call into an asynchronous one: the producer returns immediately, and a consumer does the slow work later - trading instant results for throughput and resilience.', 14),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Splitting distinct rows across database nodes by a shard key is called...","options":["replication","indexing","sharding","caching"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sharding partitions the data so each node holds a slice, growing capacity."},{"prompt":"Finding one row among about 1 million using a balanced index takes roughly how many comparisons?","options":["about 20","about 1,000","about 500,000","about 1,000,000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"log2(1,000,000) is about 20, versus a million for a full scan."},{"prompt":"During a network partition, CAP forces a choice between...","options":["consistency and availability","latency and durability","sharding and replication","reads and writes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Partition tolerance is required, leaving a tradeoff between consistency and availability."},{"prompt":"A social feed''s like-count is usually a good fit for...","options":["strong consistency","eventual consistency","no database at all","a single global lock"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brief disagreement in the count is harmless, so eventual consistency keeps the system available."},{"prompt":"Under at-least-once delivery, consumers must be...","options":["stateful","idempotent","single-threaded","synchronous"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The same message may arrive twice, so processing must be safe to repeat."},{"prompt":"A backlog of 600,000 messages drained by consumers at 200 per second in 500 seconds needs how many consumers?","options":["3","6","12","60"],"answer":1,"explanation":"600,000 / 500 = 1,200 per second, and 1,200 / 200 = 6 consumers."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Designing APIs and rate limiting for scale', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Shape a clean API for scale, paginate large results, and apply rate limiting to protect shared capacity. Warm-up: what stops a single client from overwhelming a shared service?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"An API is the contract between clients and your system. For scale, design around resources with consistent naming, return only what is needed, and never return an unbounded list - use pagination with a limit and a cursor so a single call cannot pull millions of rows. Version the contract so changes do not break existing clients. Rate limiting caps how many requests a client may make in a time window, protecting the system from spikes and abuse. A common method is the token bucket: each client has a bucket that refills at a steady rate up to a maximum, and each request spends one token; when the bucket is empty, further requests are rejected until it refills. Return a clear status and a retry hint so well-behaved clients back off."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Pagination sizing: returning 1,000,000 rows in one response is unworkable, so a page limit of 100 with a cursor yields 10,000 pages fetched on demand, and clients stop when done. Token bucket math: a bucket of capacity 100 refilling at 10 tokens per second lets a client burst 100 requests immediately, then settle to 10 per second sustained. Global capacity: if the service handles 50,000 requests per second and you have 10,000 clients, a fair steady share is about 5 per second each, so a per-client limit near that with some burst headroom protects the whole. Backoff: a rejected client told to retry after 2 seconds spreads its load instead of hammering."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) With a page limit of 50, how many pages cover 10,000 items? 2) A token bucket of capacity 60 refills at 1 per second; how many requests can a client make in an instant burst? 3) After that burst, what sustained rate can the client keep? 4) Why should a list endpoint never return an unbounded result? 5) What should a rate-limited response include so clients behave well? Answers: 1) 10,000 / 50 = 200 pages. 2) Up to 60 requests, the bucket capacity. 3) 1 request per second, the refill rate. 4) A single call could pull millions of rows and overload the system and client. 5) A clear rejection status and a retry-after hint so the client backs off."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: an endpoint that returns everything with no pagination, which melts down as data grows. Mistake: rate limiting with a hard cutoff and no retry guidance, so clients retry immediately and worsen the spike. Exit: (a) What technique bounds a large result set across calls? (b) In a token bucket, what does the refill rate control? Answers: pagination with a limit and cursor; the sustained request rate a client can maintain."}]}'::jsonb, 'A good API is a stable contract: predictable resources, clear pagination, and explicit limits, so clients can be written once and keep working as the system grows.', 14),
  (11, 'Reliability: redundancy, failover, and monitoring', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Increase reliability with redundancy and failover, and measure health with monitoring and clear targets. Warm-up: if a service must survive one server dying, what is the minimum number of servers to run?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Reliability is the property that a system keeps working correctly despite failures. Redundancy removes single points of failure by running multiple copies of each critical component, so the loss of one does not stop the whole. Failover is the automatic switch to a healthy standby when the active component dies, for example promoting a replica to primary. Monitoring watches the system through metrics, logs, and alerts, so operators learn of trouble before users do. Availability is often stated in nines: 99.9 percent allows about 8.8 hours of downtime per year, while 99.99 percent allows about 52 minutes. Track service level indicators such as error rate and latency against explicit targets, and alert when they breach."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Downtime math: 99.9 percent availability means 0.1 percent of a year is downtime; a year is about 8,760 hours, so 0.001 x 8,760 is about 8.8 hours per year. At 99.99 percent it is about 0.88 hours, roughly 52 minutes. Redundancy sizing: to survive one failure while serving peak on 12 servers, run 13 so a loss still leaves 12. Failover in practice: a primary database dies, monitoring detects the missed health check, and a replica is promoted so writes resume. Combining independent components: if a request needs two services each up 99.9 percent, the combined availability is about 0.999 x 0.999, near 99.8 percent - chains of dependencies lower the total."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) About how many hours of yearly downtime does 99.9 percent availability allow? 2) To survive one failure while serving peak on 20 servers, how many should you run? 3) What is the automatic switch to a standby called? 4) Two independent services at 99.9 percent each give roughly what combined availability? 5) Name one signal monitoring should track against a target. Answers: 1) About 8.8 hours per year. 2) 21 servers, so a loss still leaves 20. 3) Failover. 4) About 99.8 percent, since 0.999 x 0.999 is about 0.998. 5) Error rate or latency (or request throughput)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: a single primary with no standby, so one failure takes the system down. Mistake: monitoring that collects metrics but never alerts, so failures are found by users first. Exit: (a) What removes single points of failure? (b) What does 99.99 percent availability allow in downtime per year, roughly? Answers: redundancy; about 52 minutes."}]}'::jsonb, 'Reliability is designed in through redundancy and detection, not bolted on; assume every component will eventually fail and plan for how the system keeps serving.', 13),
  (12, 'Worked design: a URL shortener', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Walk a full design for a URL shortener, applying requirements, estimates, storage, caching, and reliability together. Warm-up: what are the two core operations a URL shortener must support?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A URL shortener maps a short code to a long URL and redirects users who visit the short link. Functional requirements: create a short code for a long URL, and redirect from a short code to the original. Non-functional: redirects must be fast and highly available, and reads vastly outnumber writes. The core data model is a table mapping code to long URL. A short code can be generated by encoding an incrementing id in base62, which uses digits and letters to keep codes short. Because redirects dominate and the mapping rarely changes, caching the code-to-URL lookup gives very high hit ratios. Redundancy and replicas keep redirects available even if a node fails."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Estimate the load: suppose 100 million new links per day and a 100-to-1 read-to-write ratio. Writes are 100,000,000 / 86,400, about 1,160 per second; reads are about 116,000 per second - clearly read-dominated, so cache aggressively. Storage: 100 million links per day at about 500 bytes each is 50 GB per day, roughly 18 TB per year, which suggests planning for sharded or partitioned storage over time. Code space: base62 with 7 characters gives 62^7, about 3.5 trillion codes, far more than years of demand. Cache impact: a 99 percent hit ratio on 116,000 reads per second sends only about 1,160 per second to the database, matching a modest replica setup. Reliability: run the redirect service redundantly behind a load balancer with database replicas for failover."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) At 100 million writes per day, estimate writes per second. 2) With a 100-to-1 read ratio, estimate reads per second. 3) Why is caching so effective for this system? 4) Roughly how many codes does 7-character base62 provide? 5) At a 99 percent cache hit ratio on 116,000 reads per second, how many reach the database? Answers: 1) 100,000,000 / 86,400 is about 1,160 per second. 2) About 116,000 per second. 3) Reads dominate and the mapping rarely changes, so hit ratios are very high. 4) 62^7, about 3.5 trillion. 5) 1 percent of 116,000, about 1,160 per second."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: designing for symmetric read and write load when redirects vastly outnumber creations. Mistake: generating random codes with no collision check, risking two long URLs sharing a code; base62 of a unique id avoids this. Exit: (a) Which operation dominates a URL shortener''s traffic? (b) What technique produces short, unique codes from an id? Answers: redirects (reads); base62 encoding of a unique incrementing id."}]}'::jsonb, 'Work top-down: requirements, estimates, data model, then components - the same sequence used earlier, now applied end to end to one system.', 15),
  (13, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A token bucket of capacity 100 refilling at 10 per second lets a client sustain what steady rate?","options":["100 per second","10 per second","1 per second","1,000 per second"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After the initial burst of 100, the sustained rate equals the refill rate, 10 per second."},{"prompt":"The safe way to return a very large list from an API is...","options":["send it all at once","paginate with a limit and cursor","compress it","cache it forever"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pagination bounds each response so no single call pulls millions of rows."},{"prompt":"99.9 percent availability allows roughly how much downtime per year?","options":["52 minutes","8.8 hours","3.65 days","5 minutes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"0.1 percent of about 8,760 hours is about 8.8 hours per year."},{"prompt":"The automatic switch to a healthy standby when a component fails is called...","options":["sharding","failover","load balancing","indexing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Failover promotes or activates a standby so service continues."},{"prompt":"A URL shortener''s traffic is dominated by...","options":["writes (creating links)","reads (redirects)","deletes","schema changes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Redirects vastly outnumber link creations, so the system is read-heavy."},{"prompt":"Two independent services each at 99.9 percent availability combine to roughly...","options":["99.99 percent","99.8 percent","99.0 percent","100 percent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"0.999 x 0.999 is about 0.998, so chained dependencies lower the total."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (14, 'Capstone design exercise with evaluation rubric', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"The task","text":"Produce a complete design for a news feed service where users follow others and see a timeline of recent posts. Do not just draw boxes - write requirements, a back-of-envelope estimate, a data model, and a justified choice for scaling, caching, consistency, and reliability, exactly as you would present in a design review."},{"heading":"The structure to follow","text":"1) Requirements: list functional operations (post, follow, read timeline) and non-functional targets (latency, availability, consistency needs). 2) Estimates: assume figures such as 50 million daily active users, an average of 200 follows, and reads far exceeding writes, then compute writes per second, reads per second, and storage per day. 3) Data model: describe how posts, follows, and timelines are stored. 4) Design decisions: choose fan-out on write versus fan-out on read for the timeline, decide where caching lives, pick a consistency model per feature, and state redundancy and failover. 5) Justify: for each decision, name the requirement or estimate that drives it and the tradeoff you accept."},{"heading":"A worked estimate to anchor you","text":"Suppose 50 million daily active users each post twice per day: that is 100 million posts per day, about 1,160 per second, with peaks several times higher. If each user reads their timeline 20 times per day, that is 1 billion reads per day, about 11,600 per second - an order of magnitude more reads than writes, which argues for caching timelines and for fan-out on write for most users so reads are cheap. For a celebrity with millions of followers, fan-out on write is too expensive, so a hybrid pulls their posts at read time. Storage: 100 million posts per day at about 1 KB each is 100 GB per day, roughly 36 TB per year, pointing to partitioned storage. Use these numbers, or your own clearly stated ones, to justify the design."},{"heading":"Self-check rubric","text":"Score each item as met or not. Requirements: functional and non-functional requirements are both listed before any component appears. Estimates: writes per second, reads per second, and storage per day are computed from stated round numbers, and the read-heavy nature is identified. Data model: posts, follows, and timelines are described clearly. Scaling: a fan-out approach is chosen with its tradeoff named, and the celebrity edge case is addressed. Caching: what is cached and how it is invalidated is stated. Consistency: a model is chosen per feature (for example, eventual consistency for the feed) with justification. Reliability: redundancy, failover, and a monitored target are named. Justification: every major decision cites the requirement or estimate that drives it. A design that meets all eight is a strong, defensible answer; any unmet item shows exactly where to sharpen the reasoning."}]}'::jsonb, 'Design is judged not by the diagram but by whether each choice is justified against stated requirements and estimates - make your reasoning explicit.', 16),
  (15, 'Comprehensive final quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The correct first step on a vague design prompt is to...","options":["choose a database engine","clarify requirements and scope","draw the final diagram","pick a programming language"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You must scope functional and non-functional requirements before designing."},{"prompt":"Adding more identical servers behind a load balancer is...","options":["vertical scaling","horizontal scaling","sharding","caching"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Scaling out with more nodes is horizontal scaling."},{"prompt":"A cache serving 90 percent of 200,000 reads per second sends how many to the database?","options":["2,000","20,000","180,000","200,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"10 percent of 200,000 = 20,000 reads per second reach the database."},{"prompt":"Copying the same data across nodes for read throughput and failover is...","options":["sharding","replication","indexing","pagination"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Replication keeps copies; sharding instead splits distinct rows."},{"prompt":"During a network partition, CAP forces you to give up...","options":["partition tolerance","either consistency or availability","durability","indexing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Partition tolerance is required, so you trade consistency against availability."},{"prompt":"Under at-least-once queue delivery, a consumer must be...","options":["stateful","idempotent","synchronous","single-threaded"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The same message may be delivered twice, so reprocessing must be harmless."},{"prompt":"A token bucket''s refill rate determines a client''s...","options":["burst capacity","sustained request rate","cache size","storage limit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Capacity sets the burst; the refill rate sets the sustained rate."},{"prompt":"99.99 percent availability allows roughly how much downtime per year?","options":["8.8 hours","52 minutes","5 minutes","3.65 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"0.01 percent of about 8,760 hours is about 0.88 hours, near 52 minutes."},{"prompt":"An index speeds a lookup among 1 million rows to roughly how many comparisons?","options":["about 20","about 1,000","about 500,000","about 1,000,000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"log2(1,000,000) is about 20, versus a million for a full scan."},{"prompt":"A URL shortener is best characterized as...","options":["write-heavy","read-heavy","balanced","compute-heavy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Redirects (reads) vastly outnumber link creations (writes)."},{"prompt":"For a news feed, a celebrity with millions of followers argues against pure...","options":["fan-out on read","fan-out on write","caching","replication"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fan-out on write to millions of timelines is too costly; a hybrid pulls their posts at read time."},{"prompt":"100 million posts per day at about 1 KB each is roughly how much storage per day?","options":["1 GB","10 GB","100 GB","1 TB"],"answer":2,"explanation":"100,000,000 x 1 KB = 100,000,000 KB, about 100 GB per day."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0002-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c' and l.position = v.pos
);
